#Xenon lines, air wavelengths (Angstrom), 2865-8232 A.  Columns: wavelength, relative intensity, optional flag
#(-1 = blended, not used in the fit), comment.
#Wavelengths: NIST ASD observed wavelengths (Xe I) and the NIST Handbook of Basic Atomic Spectroscopic Data
#strong-line tables (Xe II, and Xe I outside 3400-5000 A).
#Intensities: between 3446 and 4606 A, measured from a GTC/OSIRIS R2500U xenon arc calibrated by superFATBOY
#(0.06 px RMS) and put on the Handbook Xe II scale by the median ratio of the Xe II lines.  Other Xe I lines in
#3400-5000 A: NIST ASD intensity x 47 (the median measured/ASD ratio of the strong Xe I lines in range) or, where ASD
#gives none (4624.28, 4671.23, 4697.02), an estimate from g*A relative to 4500.98, corrected by 0.69 (the
#measured/predicted ratio for 4524.68 and 4582.75).  Elsewhere the Handbook intensities are kept.
#A xenon lamp is mostly neutral: its Xe I lines are 30-150 times brighter than its Xe II lines, which the Handbook
#scale (Xe II from a different source) hides.  Relative intensities still depend on the lamp and the instrument
#throughput; superFATBOY writes the intensities it measures in your own data to wavelengthCalibrated/measured_lines_*.dat.
